---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Src from '../components/Src.astro';
import Aff from '../components/Aff.astro';
import { S } from '../data/sources.js';
const title = 'Does Junk Mail Count Toward Your Virtual Mailbox Limit? Provider Rules Compared';
const description = 'Junk mail is free at Traveling Mailbox, Stable, LegalZoom Virtual Mail, Virtual Post Mail and most iPostal1 locations, but counts toward your limit at PostScan Mail ($0.50 per extra item). Each provider’s rule and overage fee, checked Nov 24, 2026.';
const schema = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":title,"description":description,"dateModified":"2026-11-24"};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated="2026-11-24" schema={schema}>
  <h1>Does junk mail count toward your virtual mailbox limit?</h1>
  <div class="answer"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> At most providers, no. Traveling Mailbox, Stable, LegalZoom Virtual Mail and Virtual Post Mail exclude junk from your monthly limit, most iPostal1 locations discard it without counting it, and US Global Mail has no mail-piece limit. PostScan Mail is the exception: junk counts, and every item over your plan costs $0.50.</p></div>
  <p class="lede">Most plans cap the pieces of mail you can receive each month, and catalogs and credit card offers can eat that cap fast. Here's how each provider treats junk, and what an extra piece costs once you're over, from each provider's own pages on November 24, 2026.</p>

  <h2>Which providers count junk mail?</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Does junk count?</th><th>How junk is handled</th><th>Cost per piece over the limit</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Traveling Mailbox</td><td>No, once you mark it as junk</td><td>You mark it as junk, and it's shredded for free <Src href={S.tmb} /></td><td>$0.25 per envelope <Src href={S.tmb} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stable</td><td>No</td><td>Obvious junk (advertisements, coupons, magazines) is filtered out and never uploaded <Src href={S.stable} /></td><td>$3 <Src href={S.stable} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>LegalZoom Virtual Mail (Earth Class Mail)</td><td>No</td><td>Plan limits are listed with "junk mail excluded from limit" <Src href={S.lzVm} /></td><td>$3 per item <Src href={S.lzVm} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Virtual Post Mail</td><td>No, for obvious junk</td><td>Obvious junk is sorted out automatically. If staff aren't sure, the item is uploaded <Src href={S.vpmPricing} /></td><td>$0.50 on Starter; Plus and up have unlimited envelopes <Src href={S.vpmPricing} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>iPostal1</td><td>No, at most locations</td><td>"Most iPostal1 locations" discard USPS Standard Mail (flyers, circulars, catalogs), and discarded junk doesn't count <Src href={S.ipFaq} /></td><td>$0.35 per item <Src href={S.ipVma} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>US Global Mail</td><td>No limit to count against</td><td>Obvious junk is pre-sorted out and shredded for free <Src href={S.usgmJunk} /></td><td>No cap on mail pieces <Src href={S.usgmMailLimit} /></td></tr>
      <tr><td>PostScan Mail</td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Counts toward your limit "so that no desired mail is mistakenly destroyed"; some addresses may filter Presorted Standard mail <Src href={S.psmFaq} /></td><td>$0.50 per item <Src href={S.psm} /></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="meta">Anytime Mailbox plans are set by each location, and its FAQ doesn't publish one junk mail rule <Src href={S.atmFaq} />. Check the plan details for the address you choose.</p>

  <h2>The fine print</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Traveling Mailbox:</strong> "anything you mark as 'Junk' is shredded for free and does not count towards your plan limits" <Src href={S.tmb} />. So it's your click that keeps it off the count.</li>
    <li><strong>Virtual Post Mail:</strong> an older help article says "any mail that shows up in your online mailbox counts toward your quota." Mail addressed to "Current Resident/Occupant" is trashed automatically, but things like a bank's debit card offer get uploaded to be safe <Src href={S.vpmJunkHelp} />. This only matters on Starter, which caps envelopes at 25 <Src href={S.vpmPricing} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Stable:</strong> Grow allows 30 pieces a month (360 a year on yearly billing) and Scale 50 (600 a year). Junk "will be filtered out" before counting <Src href={S.stable} />.</li>
    <li><strong>US Global Mail:</strong> doesn't limit incoming mail, but says accounts with "unusually high volumes" may be reviewed <Src href={S.usgmMailLimit} />.</li>
    <li><strong>iPostal1:</strong> the rule applies at "most" locations, not all <Src href={S.ipFaq} />.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What does junk cost on a capped plan?</h2>
  <p>Say you get 40 pieces a month and 15 of them are junk.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>PostScan Mail Starter</strong> (30 items, from $10/mo): all 40 count, so 10 over × $0.50 = $5 a month extra <Src href={S.psm} /> <Src href={S.psmFaq} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Stable Grow</strong> (30 pieces): 25 count, so no overage <Src href={S.stable} />.</li>
    <li><strong>iPostal1 $9.99 plan</strong> (30 items): if your location discards the junk, 25 count and there's no overage. If it doesn't, 10 × $0.35 = $3.50 <Src href={S.ipVma} /> <Src href={S.ipFaq} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Traveling Mailbox Basic</strong> (40 envelopes): within the limit either way <Src href={S.tmb} />.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How can you cut junk at the source?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prescreened credit and insurance offers:</strong> the CFPB says you can opt out at OptOutPrescreen.com or by calling 1-888-5-OPTOUT. A phone or online opt-out lasts five years; a permanent opt-out needs a signed form by mail <Src href={S.cfpbOptOut} label="CFPB" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't forward advertising:</strong> a USPS change of address doesn't forward USPS Marketing Mail unless the sender has paid for forwarding <Src href={S.uspsForward} label="USPS" />. If you <a href="/change-address-to-virtual-mailbox/">move your mail to a virtual mailbox</a> that way, most bulk advertising stays behind.</li>
  </ul>

